  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nMontana will be in SW flow aloft today with upper low over the\nPacific NW and upper ridge to the east. A humid airmass will\ncombine with the moist SW flow aloft today to create an unstable\natmosphere which will allow for chances for showers and\nthunderstorms at times. A few storms could become severe with\nlarge hail and damaging winds. It looks like the Yellowstone \nValley and points east will have another hot day with highs in the\n90s. \n\nSurface low develops over SE Montana this afternoon and tonight.\nMeanwhile, a wave on SW flow aloft tonight will allow widespread\nrain with scattered thunderstorms to develop with heavy rain\npossible at times. The upper low moves into Montana on Monday as\nthe surface low moves north into Saskatchewan. Rain is expected to\ncontinue most of the time in the north while the models differ on\nhow much the dry slot will limit rain in the south. The upper low\nmoves to Saskatchewan and Manitoba Monday night and becomes\nstacked with the surface low. Gusty west winds will develop during\nthe day on Monday and continue Monday night and Tuesday with gusts\nto 40 to 50 mph possible. A Lake Wind Advisory will most \ndefinitely be needed for Monday night into Tuesday. Wrap around \nrain is likely Monday night and Tuesday over the NW half of the \nforecast area while the SE will be dry most of the time. \n\nRainfall totals 1 to 2 inches are likely in the NW between tonight\nand Tuesday while some areas in the SE could end up with less than\na tenth of an inch. \n\nThe system exits the region Tuesday night and Wednesday and\nThursday are expected to be dry. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nThere is moderate to high (50 to 80 percent) confidence in rain \namounts from tonight through Tuesday, and winds for Monday\nafternoon through Tuesday.\n\nThere is low confidence in the showers and thunderstorm chances\ntoday. \n\nTweaked pops at times tonight to provide better hour to hour\nconsistency.",
